1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,770
How would you survive without your smartphone?
如果没有智能手机，你该如何生活？

2
00:00:03,770 --> 00:00:10,620
For many of us, it’s the gadget we’re most hooked on – looking at it hundreds of times a day.
对我们很多人来说，智能手机是最让我们着迷的设备——每天要看上数百次。

3
00:00:10,620 --> 00:00:17,860
We depend on it to perform a multitude of tasks and to connect with our friends and family.
我们依靠它完成各种各样的任务，以及与亲友保持联系。

4
00:00:17,860 --> 00:00:21,700
But have we become addicted to our phones?
但我们是否已经对手机上瘾了呢？

5
00:00:21,700 --> 00:00:29,080
Certainly, the inventor of the first mobile phone, American engineer Martin Cooper, thinks we might be.
第一部移动电话的发明者、美国工程师马丁·库珀认为我们可能已经上瘾了。

6
00:00:29,080 --> 00:00:35,640
In a BBC interview, he suggested people should stop scrolling and “get a life”.
他在接受BBC采访时建议人们应该停止刷屏，“做点有意义的事情”。

7
00:00:35,640 --> 00:00:43,260
But of course, once we start doom scrolling or watching videos, we just can’t kick the habit.
但当然，一旦我们开始“末日刷屏”或是看视频，就很难戒掉这个习惯。

8
00:00:43,260 --> 00:00:59,650
Psychologist and author Jean Twenge says we’re all guilty of “compulsively checking [our] phone if [we’re] waiting for a text or getting really into social media then kind of, looking up and realising that an hour has passed.”
心理学家兼作家简·特温吉表示，我们都有这样的毛病：“等短信的时候会忍不住频繁查看手机，或者沉迷社交媒体，抬头时才发现一个小时已经过去了。”

9
00:00:59,650 --> 00:01:08,720
There’s even a word – a phubber – to describe a person who ignores the real people around them because they are concentrating on their phones.
甚至还有一个专门的词——“低头族”，用来形容那些因为专注于手机而忽视身边人的人。

10
00:01:08,720 --> 00:01:12,570
But does it matter if we make the most of this technology?
但我们充分利用这项技术，真的有问题吗？

11
00:01:12,570 --> 00:01:19,400
Possibly, because like a drug, the problem arises when it is withdrawn.
或许是有的，因为就像毒品一样，当手机被拿走时，问题就出现了。

12
00:01:19,400 --> 00:01:27,660
A study from King’s College London found young people they studied couldn’t control the amount of time they spend on their phone.
伦敦国王学院的一项研究发现，参与调查的年轻人无法控制自己使用手机的时长。

13
00:01:27,660 --> 00:01:39,260
Such addictive behaviour means that people become ‘panicky’ or ‘upset’ if they are denied constant access, which can cause anxiety and mental health issues.
这种成瘾行为意味着，当人们无法随时使用手机时，会变得“恐慌”或“烦躁”，进而可能引发焦虑和心理健康问题。

14
00:01:39,260 --> 00:01:50,770
Interestingly, another study by the London School of Economics and Political Science suggests we don’t just look at our phones because we are prompted to by a text or email.
有趣的是，另一项由伦敦政治经济学院进行的研究表明，我们看手机不只是因为收到了短信或邮件的提醒。

15
00:01:50,770 --> 00:01:58,720
The people they studied felt automatically urged to interact with their phone, just as a smoker would light a cigarette.
参与研究的人表示，自己会下意识地想要去碰手机，就像吸烟者会习惯性地点烟一样。

16
00:01:58,720 --> 00:02:11,760
Prof Saadi Lahlou, co-author of the study, told the BBC: “We must learn tricks to avoid the temptation when we want to concentrate or have good social relations.”
该研究的合著者萨阿迪·拉卢教授告诉BBC：“当我们想要集中注意力或是维持良好的社交关系时，必须学会一些技巧来抵御这种诱惑。”

17
00:02:11,760 --> 00:02:18,960
One solution could be an app that rewards students for time spent away from their phones.
一种解决办法是开发一款应用，奖励那些放下手机的学生。

18
00:02:18,960 --> 00:02:26,260
Another option is converting your smartphone to a dumbphone that has none of the intoxicating distractions on it.
另一种选择是把智能手机改成功能机，这类手机没有那些让人沉迷的干扰项。

19
00:02:26,260 --> 00:02:32,480
But mainly, perhaps, we just need to look up more and reconnect with the real world!
但或许，最主要的办法还是我们需要多抬头看看，重新与现实世界建立联系！
